WEBVTT

00:01.000 --> 00:11.000
Hello everyone. My name is Michael and I will tell you how to use secure boot on

00:11.000 --> 00:18.120
on the ROC chip RK3588.

00:18.120 --> 00:20.600
And I have technical difficulties.

00:35.000 --> 00:40.980
For secure boot on RK3588, you have to generate a key.

00:40.980 --> 00:48.020
sign your image burn the hash of the key to your hardware and that's it secure boot is enabled

00:48.740 --> 00:49.940
thank you for your attention

00:56.260 --> 01:03.860
i see that i have some time left so maybe we look a bit closer and i will tell you about how secure

01:03.860 --> 01:10.800
Secure Boot works on RK3588, how to actually assign the firmware image, how to enable Secure

01:10.800 --> 01:17.320
Boot, and finish with some recommendations and further resources for reading into it.

01:17.320 --> 01:19.380
That's me.

01:19.380 --> 01:24.700
Secure Boot or Verify Boot is a set of verification processes designed to ensure the integrity

01:24.700 --> 01:28.420
and legitimacy of the firmware running on a device.

01:28.420 --> 01:32.200
I stole that definition from the Rockchip Secure Boot Developer

01:32.200 --> 01:33.860
Guide.

01:33.860 --> 01:38.560
So for Secure Boot, we have some image.

01:38.560 --> 01:43.780
In this case, I have a Rockchip image with a key in it,

01:43.780 --> 01:47.180
a signature on that, and a bootloader inside.

01:49.780 --> 01:54.560
The SOC during boot, it runs in boot ROM.

01:54.560 --> 01:59.320
For Secure Boot, it reads the hash, we mentioned it before,

01:59.320 --> 02:01.080
from Secure OTP.

02:01.080 --> 02:03.220
That's eFusers that you burn once,

02:03.220 --> 02:06.560
and then you can't change them anymore.

02:06.560 --> 02:11.560
On RK3588, these are only accessible from the Secure

02:11.560 --> 02:13.620
World or EL3.

02:13.620 --> 02:17.900
Details don't matter, but that will be important later on.

02:17.900 --> 02:22.320
It compares the hash in the OTP area

02:22.320 --> 02:27.960
with a hash over the public key that's contained in the image.

02:27.960 --> 02:34.080
Use that public key to verify the signature of the image.

02:34.080 --> 02:38.440
Use a hash in the header to verify the bootloader

02:38.440 --> 02:41.560
and execute the actual bootloader.

02:41.560 --> 02:43.440
We can't do anything about this.

02:43.440 --> 02:47.880
So everything we have here is in the boot ROM provided

02:47.880 --> 02:49.100
by Robchip.

02:49.100 --> 02:52.080
And we don't have a chance to change it.

02:52.080 --> 02:53.380
We have to trust the boot ROM.

02:53.380 --> 02:56.280
We have to trust the OTP that it's really

02:56.280 --> 03:01.920
writable only once and that it's not changeable during boot.

03:01.920 --> 03:07.380
What we can change is how we get the key, the signature,

03:07.380 --> 03:12.720
and the hash into the OTP area.

03:12.720 --> 03:17.760
So in the factory or on your build server,

03:17.760 --> 03:20.480
you have a key pair.

03:20.480 --> 03:23.380
And from there, you take the public key

03:23.380 --> 03:26.400
and put it into the image and use the private key

03:26.400 --> 03:27.920
to sign the image.

03:27.920 --> 03:30.560
The thing that has to be secret in this case

03:30.560 --> 03:34.340
is the private key you see in purple on the slides.

03:34.340 --> 03:38.940
And you have to somehow get the hash of the public key

03:38.940 --> 03:40.820
into your device.

03:40.820 --> 03:43.340
That's also something that you have

03:43.340 --> 03:44.800
to do in a secure environment.

03:44.800 --> 03:47.040
So it has to happen in your factory.

03:47.040 --> 03:50.920
you have to have full control over this step.

03:50.920 --> 03:53.460
Otherwise, someone may change and write

03:53.460 --> 03:55.140
some different hash into your device,

03:55.140 --> 03:56.920
and secure boot is broken.

04:00.540 --> 04:05.500
So first, let's look into how image signing works.

04:05.500 --> 04:09.240
Of course, ROGchip provides a downstream solution for that.

04:09.240 --> 04:13.880
It uses MKImage to generate the firmware image.

04:13.880 --> 04:17.880
And then you get the RK sign tool, which

04:17.880 --> 04:23.340
generates the key pair and allows you to assign an image.

04:23.340 --> 04:27.940
This approach is supported by Rockchip, of course.

04:27.940 --> 04:31.860
But this RK sign tool is binary only.

04:31.860 --> 04:35.560
So we don't know what's going on in there.

04:35.560 --> 04:37.200
We may trust it.

04:37.200 --> 04:39.400
We are not sure.

04:39.400 --> 04:43.840
The RK-Sign tool is also a bit clumsy to use.

04:43.840 --> 04:48.280
So you run it several times, and in the background

04:48.280 --> 04:50.960
it generates a configuration file.

04:50.960 --> 04:54.420
So that's not something that's easy to integrate

04:54.420 --> 04:57.380
into your build tools or your build chain.

04:57.380 --> 05:01.880
And I'm personally not sure if the RK-Sign tool supports

05:01.880 --> 05:02.740
HSM support.

05:02.740 --> 05:05.200
So I saw a few hints, but I never

05:05.200 --> 05:10.060
was able to actually work with some HSM.

05:10.060 --> 05:15.940
So that's the question.

05:15.940 --> 05:21.220
With Bearbox, we use OpenSSL to generate the key pair.

05:21.220 --> 05:24.340
I think we can kind of trust OpenSSL

05:24.340 --> 05:28.240
to actually provide proper keys.

05:28.240 --> 05:33.860
And Bearbox provides an open source tool, RKImage,

05:33.860 --> 05:35.680
which generates the image.

05:35.680 --> 05:38.240
And in the same step, it also signs the image.

05:38.240 --> 05:41.360
So once you build a bare box with a configuration

05:41.360 --> 05:45.560
for secure boot, you will get your final image already

05:45.560 --> 05:47.320
signed.

05:47.320 --> 05:50.200
The advantage here is all the tools are open source.

05:50.200 --> 05:53.180
So you can look at the source code, modify it if necessary,

05:53.180 --> 05:54.880
and build it yourself.

05:54.880 --> 06:00.740
The RK image tool supports PKCS11 for talking

06:00.740 --> 06:04.240
to any HSM that provides this interface

06:04.240 --> 06:05.700
for retrieving your private key.

06:05.700 --> 06:10.280
So you can use that to get better security

06:10.280 --> 06:13.040
on your private keys and move it out

06:13.040 --> 06:15.200
of a built infrastructure.

06:15.200 --> 06:20.360
The disadvantages, it's not supported by ROC chip.

06:20.360 --> 06:23.400
If that's a disadvantage or an advantage, I don't know.

06:23.400 --> 06:24.980
You have to decide.

06:24.980 --> 06:29.380
And it may lack features that are provided by ROC chip.

06:29.380 --> 06:33.400
I'm not sure if there is anything missing from RK Image.

06:33.400 --> 06:37.960
I implemented what I needed and ignored all the other features.

06:37.960 --> 06:43.940
You may need more or something that's provided by Roger.

06:43.940 --> 06:49.280
For the board lockdown, the downstream solution,

06:49.280 --> 06:52.460
I told you OTP access is only possible

06:52.460 --> 06:55.140
from the secure world or EL3.

06:55.140 --> 07:05.100
So in UBOT, UBOT needs to write the hash in the early boot.

07:05.100 --> 07:08.500
It receives the key from the device tree.

07:08.500 --> 07:13.000
So you put your public key into the device tree.

07:13.000 --> 07:18.480
And during boot, UBOT calculates the hash on this public key,

07:18.480 --> 07:23.240
and it writes the hash into the OTP area.

07:23.240 --> 07:27.260
Problem with that is you require a separate image that

07:27.260 --> 07:31.380
carries the public key in the device tree

07:31.380 --> 07:33.860
for your device bootstrapping.

07:33.860 --> 07:36.480
That's something you can do, but in this case,

07:36.480 --> 07:39.360
you have a separate image in your factory

07:39.360 --> 07:42.100
that you have to manage.

07:42.100 --> 07:50.300
And because the OTP is not accessible from the normal world

07:50.300 --> 07:54.560
or other exception levels.

07:54.560 --> 07:57.840
You can't check if your board has secure boot enabled

07:57.840 --> 08:01.220
or which hash is written into the OTP area.

08:01.220 --> 08:04.640
So you have no information if secure boot is actually on.

08:09.620 --> 08:15.380
In bare box, the lockdown works slightly different.

08:15.380 --> 08:18.980
You calculate the hash on your build machine.

08:18.980 --> 08:23.540
So the public key hash is something that can be public,

08:23.540 --> 08:26.300
so you can carry it around.

08:26.300 --> 08:28.640
You have to ensure the integrity of this hash,

08:28.640 --> 08:32.000
but that's a different issue.

08:32.000 --> 08:35.580
And on the device in Bearbox, you

08:35.580 --> 08:40.140
have a separate command that you can run, give it this hash,

08:40.140 --> 08:42.420
and it will enable a secure boot.

08:42.420 --> 08:47.220
So you can use, at runtime, a command

08:47.220 --> 08:52.180
to enable secure boot on your devices.

08:52.180 --> 08:58.740
And since the OTP area is not accessible from the normal world,

08:58.740 --> 09:01.620
it has to go through Opti.

09:01.620 --> 09:06.360
And Opti will actually write the hash to OTP.

09:06.360 --> 09:08.620
So there are two parts in that.

09:08.620 --> 09:12.940
one in bare box for talking to Opti as a user interface

09:12.940 --> 09:17.460
and an Opti PTA to actually enable a secure boot

09:17.460 --> 09:18.000
on the device.

09:20.740 --> 09:26.720
This approach needs scripts during your manufacturing.

09:26.720 --> 09:28.960
So at one step in your manufacturing,

09:28.960 --> 09:33.900
you run your system, check secure boot is not enabled,

09:33.900 --> 09:38.100
then run the command with your hash, enable secure boot,

09:38.100 --> 09:41.260
And after that, only Secure Boot will be enabled.

09:41.260 --> 09:45.600
You have the advantage to be able to exactly determine

09:45.600 --> 09:48.220
when this will happen during your manufacturing.

09:48.220 --> 09:51.200
So you can do stuff before, enable Secure Boot,

09:51.200 --> 09:53.340
and do stuff afterwards.

09:53.340 --> 09:57.480
That's your responsibility here.

09:57.480 --> 10:01.240
And the second part is you need OPTI support

10:01.240 --> 10:03.540
with the RK Secure PTA.

10:03.540 --> 10:06.820
You need an upstream OPTI for that.

10:06.820 --> 10:09.620
So it won't work with the OPTI provided by ROC chip.

10:13.240 --> 10:16.620
So to come back to the first slide,

10:16.620 --> 10:20.140
you use OpenSSL to generate the key pair.

10:20.140 --> 10:22.600
It's an RSA key.

10:22.600 --> 10:25.780
You configure your bare box build

10:25.780 --> 10:28.000
to assign the image with this key,

10:28.000 --> 10:32.860
and you enable the RKA secure command to talk to OPTI.

10:32.860 --> 10:34.940
You build your image.

10:34.940 --> 10:39.640
You use this RKOTP script to calculate your key hash.

10:39.640 --> 10:42.920
You can pass your key there, or you can also

10:42.920 --> 10:46.080
pass an image there, and it will extract the public key

10:46.080 --> 10:48.600
and give you the hash for that.

10:48.600 --> 10:52.940
And you boot barebox and run the RK secure command,

10:52.940 --> 10:57.000
and secure boot will be enabled.

10:59.680 --> 11:01.960
Some recommendations.

11:01.960 --> 11:05.980
Store your private signing keys on HSM.

11:05.980 --> 11:10.240
Hardware secure module, no question about that.

11:10.240 --> 11:13.900
If you enable secure boot, check your public root key hash

11:13.900 --> 11:16.000
before you program it into your device.

11:16.000 --> 11:19.360
You will have a brick otherwise.

11:19.360 --> 11:21.480
And because of that, you want to test

11:21.480 --> 11:23.180
on expandable hardware first.

11:23.180 --> 11:26.500
So if you only have one device on your desk,

11:26.500 --> 11:30.960
I wouldn't try to enable secure boot there.

11:30.960 --> 11:33.900
And after you enable Secure Boot,

11:33.900 --> 11:37.980
you would want to test if unsigned images or images

11:37.980 --> 11:41.460
that are signed with a different key are rejected,

11:41.460 --> 11:43.920
to be really sure that Secure Boot is enabled.

11:46.920 --> 11:52.220
A few further resources, the GitHub repository

11:52.220 --> 11:53.620
by DualTag.

11:53.620 --> 11:56.480
I don't know how to pronounce that.

11:56.480 --> 11:58.680
They did a lot of reverse engineering,

11:58.680 --> 12:02.740
So everything I know about Secure Boot on RK3588,

12:02.740 --> 12:04.860
I know from that repository.

12:04.860 --> 12:08.960
And I worked on building it into the upstream projects

12:08.960 --> 12:13.040
to make it useful or usable.

12:13.040 --> 12:17.580
Another resource is the Rockchip Developer Guide.

12:17.580 --> 12:21.240
It will partly document what's happening

12:21.240 --> 12:23.800
in the early Secure Boot.

12:23.800 --> 12:26.400
And it will also talk about how Rockchip recommends

12:26.400 --> 12:30.640
to sign Linux or sign the root FS

12:30.640 --> 12:32.940
and have the secure boot continuing

12:32.940 --> 12:35.160
after your bootloader loaded.

12:35.160 --> 12:38.280
I didn't cover that because that's something you,

12:38.280 --> 12:40.860
once you have a trusted bootloader running,

12:40.860 --> 12:45.460
you can decide how to continue further in your boot chain

12:45.460 --> 12:49.500
and ensure secure boot there.

12:49.500 --> 12:52.020
Documentation of barebox documents,

12:52.020 --> 12:53.700
the process how to enable secure boot.

12:53.700 --> 12:55.980
So what I told you in this talk, you

12:55.980 --> 12:59.700
can find in the documentation as well in smaller steps

12:59.700 --> 13:02.340
and what to enable and how to enable it.

13:02.340 --> 13:08.540
And you can find the RK secure PTA in upstream OptiOS

13:08.540 --> 13:09.880
in this repository.

13:09.880 --> 13:13.040
So in the latest Opti releases, you already

13:13.040 --> 13:22.140
have the PTA that's necessary for secure boot, of course.

13:22.140 --> 13:25.880
So now, actually, thank you.

13:25.880 --> 13:30.740
If you have any questions, meet me later in the evening

13:30.740 --> 13:34.000
because we won't have time for questions here.

13:34.000 --> 13:36.020
If you have questions about my design decisions,

13:36.020 --> 13:38.360
I'm very welcome to hear about that.

13:38.360 --> 13:41.220
If you want to implement that in U-Board, also talk to me.

13:41.220 --> 13:44.140
I have an implementation for Bearbox, but not in U-Board.

13:44.140 --> 13:47.180
If you want to implement that in U-Board, come to me.

13:47.180 --> 13:49.720
And see you today in the evening.

13:49.720 --> 13:50.720
Thank you.

13:55.880 --> 14:25.860
Thank you.

